{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $p=-\\dfrac65$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5p+6$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{2p + 1}{5p + 6} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{7}\n\\\\\\\\\n(5p+6)\\cdot \\dfrac{2p + 1}{5p + 6} \u0026= (5p+6)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{7}\n\\\\\\\\\n2p+1\u0026=\\dfrac57 p+\\dfrac67\n\\\\\\\\\n\\dfrac97 p\u0026=-\\dfrac17\n\\\\\\\\\np\u0026=-\\dfrac19\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $p=-\\dfrac19$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**$p = -\\dfrac19$**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $p$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{2p + 1}{5p + 6} = \\dfrac{1}{7}$\n\n$p =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"answerForms":["proper","improper"],"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":-0.1111111111111111}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
